    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.all_pairs_shortest_path_length -\n    EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.all_pairs_shortest_path_length.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.all_pairs_shortest_path_length()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute shortest path lengths for all pairs of nodes.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.all_pairs_shortest_path_length()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute shortest path lengths for all pairs of nodes.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.all_pairs_shortest_path_length()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n  .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute shortest path lengths for all pairs of nodes.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute shortest path lengths for all pairs of nodes.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L60390-L60515){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.all_pairs_shortest_path_length()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![All-pairs shortest path lengths](../assets/graphs/all-pairs-shortest-path-length.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A four-node directed weighted cycle A, B, C, D with a direct shortcut A to C of weight 4. The distance matrix lists every source-target cost; the highlighted cell A to C is 3, because the route through B (1 + 2, in the accent color) beats the direct edge.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nRuns Dijkstra's algorithm from every node and records the shortest-path distance to each\nnode it can reach. On a weighted graph the edge weights are summed along each path; on an\nunweighted graph every edge counts as `1.0`, so distances are hop counts. On a directed\ngraph edges are only followed in their own direction, so `result[u][v]` and\n`result[v][u]` can differ, or one of them can be missing entirely.\n\nHaving the whole distance table at once is useful for:\n\n- answering many pairwise distance lookups without rerunning a search each time\n- building a distance matrix for clustering, embedding, or custom metrics\n- spotting which pairs cannot reach each other in disconnected or directed graphs\n\nEvery node appears as an outer key, and each inner dictionary includes the node itself at\ndistance `0.0`. Pairs with no connecting path are omitted rather than stored as infinity.\nEdge weights must be non-negative, since Dijkstra can give wrong answers otherwise (and\non an undirected graph a negative edge is a negative cycle, so shortest paths aren't\ndefined). A negative weight raises a `ValueError`; use `bellman_ford_shortest_path()`\nfor directed graphs with negative weights. The method does not modify the graph.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [dict[int, dict[int, float]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Nested dictionary where `result[source][target] = distance`, keyed by node ID. Each\n    source maps to itself with distance `0.0`. Only reachable pairs are included;\n    unreachable pairs are omitted.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If any edge has a negative weight (Dijkstra requires non-negative weights).\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nRuns Dijkstra from every node, giving $O(n(m + n)\\log n)$ time and $O(n^2)$ space\nto hold the full distance matrix. Unreachable pairs simply do not appear in the\nresult, so the returned dict may be sparse for disconnected or directed graphs. On\ngraphs where individual queries are the whole workload, calling `shortest_path()` on\ndemand is usually cheaper; the payoff of computing everything up-front is when many\npairs are needed or when the same distances feed several downstream metrics.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~all_pairs_shortest_path`) \n\n:   The paths themselves.\n\n[](`~floyd_warshall`) \n\n:   All-pairs distances, from a dense algorithm.\n\n[](`~johnson`) \n\n:   All-pairs paths with negative weights.\n\n[](`~mean_distance`) \n\n:   The average distance over all pairs.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nOn an unweighted path `1-2-3-4`, distances are hop counts, so the two ends are `3.0`\napart and node `2` is `2.0` away from node `4`:\n\n::: {#009e8820 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(4)\n\ndistances = graph.all_pairs_shortest_path_length()\ndistances[1][4], distances[2][4]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(3.0, 2.0)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nOn a weighted graph, the distance is the cheapest total weight, which need not be the\nroute with the fewest edges. Here the two-edge route through `B` beats the direct edge:\n\n::: {#25b044f7 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\", 1.0), (\"B\", \"C\", 2.0), (\"A\", \"C\", 5.0)]\n)\n\ndistances = graph.all_pairs_shortest_path_length()\ndistances[graph.get_id_from_label(\"A\")][graph.get_id_from_label(\"C\")]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n3.0\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIn a directed graph, distances follow edge direction. Relabeling the table makes it\neasy to see that `A` reaches everything while `C` only reaches itself:\n\n::: {#1f5d7c15 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\")], directed=True)\n\nlabel = graph.get_label_from_id\n{\n    label(s): {label(t): d for t, d in row.items()}\n    for s, row in graph.all_pairs_shortest_path_length().items()\n}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n{'A': {'A': 0.0, 'C': 2.0, 'B': 1.0},\n 'C': {'C': 0.0},\n 'B': {'C': 1.0, 'B': 0.0}}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nNodes in different components cannot reach each other, so those pairs are absent from\nthe result:\n\n::: {#1d3161a1 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(3)\ngraph.add_node(4)\n\ndistances = graph.all_pairs_shortest_path_length()\n1 in distances, 4 in distances[1]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n(True, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### The longest trip from each station\n\n`metro()` is a made-up city's metro network, with each stretch of track weighted by its\ntravel time in minutes. The longest of the fastest trips from each station is shortest\nfrom Central and Opera, at 14 minutes, while the Airport and Hillcrest, in opposite\ncorners, are 28 minutes apart:\n\n::: {#699ed52e .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro = ec.metro()\nminutes = metro.all_pairs_shortest_path_length()\n\nlongest = {n: max(row.values()) for n, row in minutes.items()}\nmetro.set_node_attrs(\"longest_trip\", longest)\n\nmetro.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", color_by=\"longest_trip\", color_scale=\"sequential\",\n    label_by=\"label\",\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-34938b06:#7dace5;--ect-4eae359b:#74a3dc;--ect-5ee57d66:#4874ab;--ect-e76ae989:#5986be;--ect-66b03f8b:#618fc7;--ect-6425850a:#0d366b;--ect-850c075a:#376299;--ect-975840b8:#86b6ef;--ect-8eff6c8a:#406ba2;--ect-f8094ac6:#79a8e1;--ect-472663e1:#6d9bd3;--ect-6826a5f9:#608ec6;--ect-9d43aea2:#5480b8;--ect-3c980f0e:#3c679e;--ect-ec386ef0:#305a91;--ect-97f76ec5:#254e84;--ect-e28c6970:#194278;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-152d685d:#5778a0;--ect-875b57dc:#51729a;--ect-17801347:#325177;--ect-65262fae:#3e5d85;--ect-f241547a:#43648b;--ect-bf50105f:#09254a;--ect-f8f129c2:#26446b;--ect-6970a796:#5d7fa7;--ect-7b967146:#2c4a71;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-34938b06:#26599c;--ect-4eae359b:#3363a4;--ect-5ee57d66:#7297c9;--ect-e76ae989:#5983ba;--ect-66b03f8b:#4d79b3;--ect-6425850a:#cde2fb;--ect-850c075a:#8bacd7;--ect-975840b8:#184f95;--ect-8eff6c8a:#7ea1d0;--ect-f8094ac6:#2c5d9f;--ect-472663e1:#3d6caa;--ect-6826a5f9:#4f7ab4;--ect-9d43aea2:#6089be;--ect-3c980f0e:#84a6d3;--ect-ec386ef0:#96b5dd;--ect-97f76ec5:#a8c4e7;--ect-e28c6970:#bad3f1;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-152d685d:#1a3e6d;--ect-875b57dc:#234572;--ect-17801347:#4f698c;--ect-65262fae:#3e5b82;--ect-f241547a:#35547d;--ect-bf50105f:#8f9eaf;--ect-f8f129c2:#617896;--ect-6970a796:#103768;--ect-7b967146:#587091;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ec